✅ 14. What Happens if wait() is Called Outside a Synchronized Block?

14.1 Short Answer

❌ No.
Calling wait() outside a synchronized block or synchronized method throws:
IllegalMonitorStateException
This is an unchecked exception (extends RuntimeException).

14.2 Why Does This Happen?

Remember one golden rule:
🔴 A thread can call wait(), notify(), or notifyAll() only if it currently owns that object's monitor (lock).
🔴 If it doesn't own the monitor,the JVM throws IllegalMonitorStateException

14.3 Incorrect Example

class Shared {
    void work() throws InterruptedException {
        wait();
    }
}

14.4 What Happens?

Suppose

Shared shared = new Shared();
shared.work();

Execution

work()
↓
wait()
↓
Thread owns monitor?
↓
NO
↓
IllegalMonitorStateException

14.5 Runtime Exception

Exception in thread "main"
java.lang.IllegalMonitorStateException

14.6 Why Doesn't the Thread Own the Lock?

Because

void work(){
    wait();
}

contains no synchronization.

The thread entered the method like any normal method.

It never acquired
shared's monitor

So

wait();
is illegal.

14.7 Summary Table

| Code                                 | Valid? | Reason               |
| ------------------------------------ | ------ | -------------------- |
| `wait()` outside synchronized        | ❌      | No monitor ownership |
| `notify()` outside synchronized      | ❌      | No monitor ownership |
| `notifyAll()` outside synchronized   | ❌      | No monitor ownership |
| `synchronized(lock){ lock.wait(); }` | ✅      | Same monitor         |
| `synchronized(this){ wait(); }`      | ✅      | Same monitor         |
| `synchronized(this){ lock.wait(); }` | ❌      | Different monitor    |
